{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-abef5194-1106-4077-ab7b-de9339a64944","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8cfda46d7b5af319","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9008-12an3_z7/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9008-E002.md","content":"# Define data-scientist onboarding scope and baseline where onboarding time is spent today\n\n## Description\n\nThe strategy sets a target — onboarding a new data scientist should take \"less\nthan a day\" — without defining what onboarding includes or measuring where the\nday is currently spent. Until the scope is defined and the current state is\nbaselined, no implementation epic can be responsibly scoped: the fix differs\ncompletely depending on whether the time goes to cluster access, project\nsetup, first workbench, or first deployed model. This investigation defines\nthe onboarding boundary, maps the current journey, and produces a per-stage\ntime breakdown with ranked bottlenecks. Its findings scope the phase-2\nimplementation epics and ground the onboarding best practices in the\nenterprise usability whitepaper.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Obtain a ratified definition of what \"onboarding a new data scientist\"\n  covers for the less-than-a-day target: which of cluster access, project\n  setup, first workbench, and first deployed model count as onboarding (a\n  product decision, recorded with rationale).\n- Map the current end-to-end onboarding journey as a new data scientist\n  experiences it — from account/project request through project creation in\n  the Dashboard console, workbench spawn, and first productive notebook\n  session — identifying the responsible platform surface for each stage\n  (Dashboard console, Notebooks/workbench tooling, customer-side or\n  platform-side administration).\n- Measure or estimate the current time spent per stage, based on the\n  documented flow, walkthroughs of a representative environment, and field\n  feedback, and rank the bottlenecks (wait time vs. manual steps vs.\n  comprehension/learning time).\n- Distinguish friction that is within platform control from friction that is\n  external (customer-side approvals, cluster capacity, procurement).\n- Produce a findings report: the ratified onboarding scope, the per-stage time\n  breakdown, the ranked bottlenecks, and candidate improvement areas with the\n  affected platform surface for each — suitable for scoping implementation\n  epics.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A ratified definition of the onboarding scope exists, stating which stages\n  (cluster access, project setup, first workbench, first deployed model) count\n  toward the less-than-a-day target.\n- The current onboarding journey is documented stage by stage, with the\n  responsible platform surface or external party identified for each stage.\n- A per-stage time breakdown exists for the current state, with the dominant\n  bottleneck(s) identified and the evidence for each figure recorded.\n- The findings explicitly separate platform-controllable friction from\n  external/customer-side friction.\n- Candidate improvement areas are enumerated, each tagged with the platform\n  surface it would touch, in enough detail to scope implementation epics.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-2 (onboarding a new data scientist should take less than a day — scope\n  undefined): this investigation defines the scope and baselines the current\n  state that HLR-2's target is measured against. Implementation epics are\n  defined in a follow-up decomposition against the ranked bottlenecks; the\n  findings also ground the whitepaper's onboarding guidance.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":47570,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9c6f096221755416","agent_id":"a146aba0fdbcda8ca","session_id":"fedb8ce9-e0a4-4a22-8d10-7c9db2355a36","uuid"