and account owners to name the specific enterprise tools\n46\t  behind the general \"integrate better\" statements (for example: identity\n47\t  providers, source control, CI systems, ticketing/work management, data\n48\t  platforms, model/ML tooling), since the consolidated feedback names none.\n49\t- Inventory RHOAI's existing enterprise integration points (for example\n50\t  OpenShift OAuth for authentication and project-scoped RBAC, workbench source\n51\t  control integration) from the platform's documentation and architecture\n52\t  context, and identify the gaps relative to the named tools.\n53\t- For each candidate tool, assess demand weight (how many accounts, which\n54\t  strategic accounts), the rough integration surface, and the platform\n55\t  component that would primarily own the work.\n56\t- Produce a prioritized target-tool list, ratified by product/stakeholder\n57\t  owners, with per-tool rationale, demand evidence, and the platform surface\n58\t  each integration would touch.\n59\t\n60\t## Acceptance Criteria\n61\t\n62\t- All integration-related feedback is consolidated into a single document with\n63\t  per-account attribution and any concrete tool mentions extracted.\n64\t- A named candidate tool list exists with per-account demand evidence and a\n65\t  rough integration surface assessment for each tool.\n66\t- RHOAI's existing enterprise integration points are inventoried, with gaps\n67\t  identified relative to the named tools.\n68\t- A prioritized target-tool list is ratified by product/stakeholder owners,\n69\t  identifying for each tool the platform component the integration would\n70\t  primarily touch — in enough detail to scope implementation epics.\n71\t\n72\t## HLR Traceability\n73\t\n74\t- HLR-3 (the platform should integrate better with existing enterprise\n75\t  tooling — no tools named): this investigation resolves which tools HLR-3\n76\t  actually refers to. Implementation epics are defined in a follow-up\n77\t  decomposition once 