{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-beebf59411afade1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9008\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9008: Make RHOAI more usable for enterprise teams\n10\t\n11\t## Approach\n12\t\n13\tThis strategy is not implementable as written: HLR-1 states two contradictory\n14\trequirements (fewer exposed configuration options vs. more fine-grained\n15\tper-project controls), HLR-2 sets a target (\"onboarding in less than a day\")\n16\twithout defining what onboarding covers or where the time is spent today, and\n17\tHLR-3 asks for \"better integration\" with enterprise tooling without naming a\n18\tsingle tool. All three carry explicit open questions in the strategy, and each\n19\tanswer changes which implementation epics exist and what they do. None of the\n20\tthree names a target state or measurable criteria, so writing implementation\n21\tepics for them now would require inventing scope the strategy does not contain.\n22\t\n23\tPer the unbounded-outcome path of the investigation-edge rule, this is a\n24\t**phased (investigation-first) decomposition**:\n25\t\n26\t- **Phase 1 (this decomposition):** one Investigation epic per genuine unknown\n27\t  (HLR-1, HLR-2, HLR-3), plus the one fully specified deliverable — the HLR-4\n28\t  whitepaper (`docs-authoring`), which consumes the investigations' findings so\n29\t  published guidance matches the resolved direction instead of the contradictory\n30\t  raw feedback.\n31\t- **Phase 2 (follow-up decomposition after the investigations resolve):**\n32\t  implementation epics for HLR-1..HLR-3, scoped from the ratified decisions.\n33\t\n34\tNo Staff Engineer Input section is present in the strategy; no overrides to log.\n35\t\n36\t## Epic List\n37\t\n38\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n39\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n40\t| RHAISTRAT-9008-E001 | Resolve conflicting administration-experience requirements and set the configuration direction | Investigation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n41\t| RHAISTRAT-9008-E002 | Define data-scientist onboarding scope and baseline where onboarding time is spent today | Investigation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n42\t| RHAISTRAT-9008-E003 | Identify and prioritize the enterprise tools behind the integration feedback | Investigation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n43\t| RHAISTRAT-9008-E004 | Publish the enterprise usability best-practices whitepaper for RHOAI | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | P2 |\n44\t\n45\t## Dependency DAG\n46\t\n47\t```mermaid\n48\tgraph TD\n49\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9008-E001] --> E004[RHAISTRAT-9008-E004]\n50\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-9008-E002] --> E004\n51\t    E003[RHAISTRAT-9008-E003] --> E004\n52\t```\n53\t\n54\tAll three Investigations are roots and run in parallel. The whitepaper is the\n55\tonly downstream node.\n56\t\n57\t## DAG Justification\n58\t\n59\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n60\t|------|------|-----------|\n61\t| E001 → E004 | Investigation gates downstream work (Rule 3); artifact consumption | The whitepaper's administration/configuration guidance cannot be written while the platform direction is self-contradictory (curated simplicity vs. per-project granularity). The administration-direction investigation produces the consolidated feedback record and ratified decision the whitepaper's guidance builds on. This is the epic's primary gate: `gated_by=E001`, `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` — if the direction decision changes or is not ratified, the administration sections are rewritten to match. |\n62\t| E002 → E004 | Investigation gates downstream work (Rule 3); artifact consumption | The onboarding best-practice sections require the defined onboarding scope and the per-stage time baseline this investigation produces; without them the whitepaper would prescribe practices for an undefined journey. |\n63\t| E003 → E004 | Investigation gates downstream work (Rule 3); artifact consumption | The enterprise-tooling integration guidance requires the named, prioritized target tools this investigation produces; the strategy names none. |\n64\t\n65\tNotes on the graph:\n66\t\n67\t- `gated_by` is single-valued in the schema; it records the primary gate (E001,\n68\t  the direction decision that most rewrites whitepaper content). E002 and E003\n69\t  are additional investigation dependencies of E004 — artifact-consumption\n70\t  edges of the same Rule 3 kind.\n71\t- There are no implementation-to-implementation edges because this phase\n72\t  contains no implementation epics for HLR-1..HLR-3 (see Approach).\n73\t- No Rule 11 (docs-last) edges are required: the strategy's only\n74\t  Implementation epic is itself the `docs-authoring` epic. When the phase-2\n75\t  implementation epics are created, they must be added to the whitepaper's\n76\t  blockers before publication (see Health Warnings).\n77\t- No parallelism rules for `repo-onboarding` / `konflux-onboarding` /\n78\t  `license-validation` apply: both affected components (Dashboard, Notebooks)\n79\t  exist in architecture context and are Konflux-onboarded; no new component is\n80\t  introduced.\n81\t\n82\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n83\t\n84\t| HLR | Priority (strategy) | Epic(s) | Coverage |\n85\t|-----|---------------------|---------|----------|\n86\t| HLR-1 — Administration should be simpler (contradictory: fewer knobs vs. more granular controls) | — (unassigned) | RHAISTRAT-9008-E001 | Investigation resolves the conflict and ratifies a direction; implementation epics are cut in the phase-2 decomposition once the direction is decided. |\n87\t| HLR-2 — Onboarding a new data scientist in less than a day (scope undefined) | — (unassigned) | RHAISTRAT-9008-E002 | Investigation defines the onboarding scope and baselines current time per stage; implementation epics follow in phase 2 against the measured bottlenecks. |\n88\t| HLR-3 — Better integration with existing enterprise tooling (no tools named) | — (unassigned) | RHAISTRAT-9008-E003 | Investigation names and prioritizes the target tools and the platform surface each touches; implementation epics follow in phase 2. |\n89\t| HLR-4 — Publish a whitepaper describing enterprise usability best practices on RHOAI | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9008-E004 | Direct coverage: the whitepaper is authored and published through the Red Hat content process. |\n90\t\n91\tAll four HLRs are covered; none is orphaned. HLR-1..HLR-3 are covered by the\n92\tinvestigation that unblocks them (their implementation work does not exist as\n93\tepics in this phase by design — see Approach), and each investigation's\n94\tfindings feed the whitepaper.\n95\t\n96\t## Health Warnings\n97\t\n98\t1. **Missing strategy sections.** The strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n99\t   Dependencies table, no Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then), no\n100\t   Non-Functional Requirements, no Staff Engineer Input, and no per-component\n101\t   change/owner detail in the Affected Components list. Owner teams were\n102\t   inferred from architecture context (Dashboard → RHOAI Dashboard; Notebooks →\n103\t   Workbenches); the whitepaper team (\"RHOAI Documentation\") is an assumption —\n104\t   confirm before scheduling.\n105\t2. **Unassigned HLR priorities.** HLR-1..HLR-3 carry no priority in the\n106\t   strategy. The three Investigations are set to P1 as the critical path to any\n107\t   usability progress (the strategy's overall priority is Normal and leadership\n108\t   wants visible progress this release). Confirm with product.\n109\t3. **Self-contradictory requirement (HLR-1).** \"Fewer configuration options\n110\t   exposed\" and \"more fine-grained controls per project\" pull in opposite\n111\t   directions. Escalated to an Investigation rather than arbitrarily picking a\n112\t   side; the resolution is a stakeholder decision, not a technical one.\n113\t4. **Scope trap.** \"Nothing has been explicitly scoped out\" combined with\n114\t   undefined scope for HLR-2 and HLR-3 risks unbounded scope growth. The\n115\t   investigations bound the scope before any implementation epic is cut.\n116\t5. **Priority-inversion risk.** HLR-4 (the P2 whitepaper) is the only\n117\t   prioritized requirement while the substantive usability work is\n118\t   unprioritized and blocked on unresolved questions. Publishing the whitepaper\n119\t   must not be treated as the release's \"visible usability progress\" while\n120\t   HLR-1..HLR-3 remain unimplemented.\n121\t6. **Source-data quality.** The feedback arrived through different channels and\n122\t   was never consolidated, and the quotes are \"partially contradictory\". All\n123\t   three investigations depend on this unconsolidated input; consolidation is\n124\t   part of each investigation's scope.\n125\t7. **Phase-2 Rule 11 obligation.** When the follow-up decomposition creates\n126\t   implementation epics for HLR-1..HLR-3, those epics must be added to the\n127\t   whitepaper epic's dependency chain before publication (docs describe what\n128\t   was built).\n129\t8. **Component mapping notes.** The onboarding investigation spans Dashboard\n130\t   and Notebooks; it is assigned to Dashboard (canonical component, RHOAI\n131\t   Dashboard team) as the console through which the whole onboarding journey\n132\t   runs, with Workbenches/Notebooks as a noted span. The tooling investigation\n133\t   is assigned to Dashboard because the Dashboard architecture context makes it\n134\t   the default user-facing integration surface; the component may be reassigned\n135\t   once the target tools are named. The Documentation component has no\n136\t   architecture-context file — treated as docs content work, no onboarding\n137\t   chain needed.\n138\t\n139\t## Tiered Delivery\n140\t\n141\tNot applicable — there is no external dependency with uncertain timing. All\n142\tgates in this decomposition are internal investigations. The relevant split is\n143\tphased, not tiered: phase 1 (this decomposition) resolves the unknowns and\n144\tpublishes the whitepaper; phase 2 implements HLR-1..HLR-3 from the ratified\n145\tdecisions.\n146\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bac7a76bcf97691f","agent_id":"a19dea9a3635e0858","session_id":"fedb8ce9-e0a4-4a22-8d10-7c9db2355a36","uuid":"ceef0530-b283-4135-a813-739f0f924c24","timestamp":"2026-10-22T18:05:43.493Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-beebf59411afade1","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9008 decomposition"}
